Welcome to Software Engineering Radio.

11
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:53,590
Thank you for having me here, Robert. It's a pleasure to be on the show.

12
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:58,389
Would you like to say anything else about your background before we dive into the topic?

13
00:00:58,399 --> 00:01:00,310
No, I think that's a great summary.

14
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:10,070
Maybe the only thing that I'll add is right now at Whisper, we're working on low latency interactive voice AI applications for users.

15
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:18,070
And so that will shed a lot of light on how we think about latency, throughput, and optimization when it comes to ML workloads.

16
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:27,030
That's a great thing for you to be working on because today we will be talking about low latency AI applications.

17
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:36,710
Before we get into latency in AI in particular, let's talk about generally in applications when we're talking about latency, what are we talking about?

18
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:45,670
So when we talk about latency, what we really care about is what the user experiences the moment they try to do something to the moment they actually experience the response.

19
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:57,270
So it's entirely from the side of the person interacting with the system, not so much from the side of like the service or the background or how much we're able to process all at once.

20
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:05,030
You could imagine a system in which a another computer program is interacting with the system.

21
00:02:05,030 --> 00:02:08,389
Does your definition rule out calling it latency in those cases?

22
00:02:08,399 --> 00:02:17,910
No. You can think about latency kind of in all sorts of cases where all you're measuring is the time from something to start to the time that something finishes.

23
00:02:17,910 --> 00:02:20,470
And so it's basically just elapsed time of some kind of operation.

24
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:31,750
And a lot of times I just tend to think about it from what the user experiences cuz that is the type of latency that a lot of software applications care to optimize.

25
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:41,030
Maybe one example that I think about is people joke about the AI pause nowadays when you're having a voice call with chat GBT voice where it pauses for three or 4 seconds.

26
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:44,949
That time span is what I think of as latency in a lot of user interactive applications.

27
00:02:44,959 --> 00:02:49,750
We'll definitely talk about that in a bit.

28
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:53,030
I want to do some more general purpose talk concepts about latency.

29
00:02:53,030 --> 00:03:07,190
We did a podcast on this some years ago that was talking about consumer applications and the guest presented some data about human behavior in response to latency showing that people are highly sensitive

30
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:13,510
to both average latency and tail latency even down into milliseconds. Now this was years ago.

31
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:21,911
Do you have any updated research or your own ideas about what humans experience as a noticeable latency? Yeah.

32
00:03:21,911 --> 00:03:24,229
So, it depends a lot on the application.

33
00:03:24,229 --> 00:03:33,110
So, there are some really prominent ones like superhuman mail, which is an email client, where what they've optimized for is the latency of every interaction.

34
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:45,190
And they found that when responding to keystrokes, for example, you click a shortcut in the application, how long until the mail app updates based on that, the target was under 100 milliseconds.

35
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:51,270
Anywhere between 50 and 100 milliseconds would feel good and anything above 100 started to feel bad very quickly.

36
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:58,630
It's a little bit different for kind of traditional software and a lot more of the AI applications and what the specific interaction is.

37
00:03:58,630 --> 00:04:03,910
So like in voice cases it tends to be 500 to 600 milliseconds tends to be tolerable.

38
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:13,429
In UIs, if you hit more than 30 to 40 milliseconds for response to like a user hitting a button, then that starts to feel laggy very very fast.

39
00:04:13,429 --> 00:04:18,310
And people can perceive latency down to like 5 or 10 milliseconds.

40
00:04:18,310 --> 00:04:27,749
Like if my keystrokes when I input them into an app lag by 10 milliseconds, I tend to use those kinds of notepad editors way less than other ones.

41
00:04:27,749 --> 00:04:37,270
Do you have any thoughts on whether people are responding to average latency, longtail latency, worst case latency or some other aggregator?

42
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:45,830
I think people tend to measure latency based on the 95th to 99th percentile experience that they have.

43
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:52,710
Like whenever at whisper people have mentioned that things feel slow when we look at the distribution of times they get it's

44
00:04:52,720 --> 00:05:02,950
actually usually one or two outlier cases where it feels slow and so people tend to measure latency based on their worst perceived experience not based on their average perceived experience.

45
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:13,110
In trying to engineer the obvious cause of average latency is the system has to do some work for you in order to give you a response and that takes time.

46
00:05:13,110 --> 00:05:20,469
you can try to make it run faster, but longtail latency may have [clears throat] different causes.

47
00:05:20,469 --> 00:05:26,070
And do you have any experience in tracking down where that originates?

48
00:05:26,070 --> 00:05:30,950
So that's where the hardest challenges in latency come from?

49
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:35,909
Because when you think about P99 latency, it's like 100 different things that can cause the issue.

50
00:05:35,909 --> 00:05:40,070
And it's about figuring out how to find those and diagnose those.

51
00:05:40,070 --> 00:05:47,270
So for us, one of the ways that we think about and analyze P99 latency is looking at the impact of the network on the user.

52
00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:54,150
Networks are super stochastic and so every once in a while, a request just won't make it where it should get to.

53
00:05:54,150 --> 00:06:04,629
And the way that we try to track these things down is by making sure that we have as much observation as possible into the breakdown of the different parts of the latency.

54
00:06:04,629 --> 00:06:07,110
So if we measure the time for the work, right?

55
00:06:07,110 --> 00:06:15,350
As you said, that's the time that every request is going to have to take and the time for every single sequence of steps, what we can do is we can figure out,

56
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:25,350
okay, this hop of network latency was the source of the issue and then we can try and figure out how do we get that 99th percentile latency to go down.

57
00:06:25,350 --> 00:06:35,350
Do you have any stories of when you looked at this telemetry and you found something maybe you weren't expecting that was taking up an normal amount of time?

58
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:38,071
Absolutely. So this is really common.

59
00:06:38,071 --> 00:06:40,950
So we host models for voice dictation.

60
00:06:40,950 --> 00:06:51,749
So basically users will press a button on their computer, they're going to speak, we send it to a server for transcription where we host all of the models ourselves and then we return a response to the user.

61
00:06:51,749 --> 00:06:54,950
And we run both a speech model and an LLM as part of that pipeline.

62
00:06:54,950 --> 00:06:57,029
That's to kind of give context on the application where we've seen it.

63
00:06:57,039 --> 00:07:04,309
The most unexpected one that has come up for us is we found that things were making it to the server fast.

64
00:07:04,309 --> 00:07:13,430
Things were getting processed by the server fast, but one day all of a sudden all of our response times went up by about 300 to 400 milliseconds.

65
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:21,510
And what we found in that case was the time between the first model running and the second model running suddenly had a spike.

66
00:07:21,510 --> 00:07:30,230
And the reason that we eventually uncovered is something in our deployment allowed one GPU to be in one region and the other GPU to be in a different region.

67
00:07:30,230 --> 00:07:33,990
So we would go to California for processing our audio.

68
00:07:33,990 --> 00:07:38,710
Then we would somehow send it to the Midwest for processing the LLM and all the post-processing.

69
00:07:38,710 --> 00:07:46,950
And this was kind of opaque behindthe-scenes infrastructure things where we thought everything was getting deployed to the same place.

70
00:07:46,950 --> 00:07:53,909
And then we looked under the hood and we found that okay there's like network responses going on here and then we were able to diagnose it and bring it all back into the same region.

71
00:07:53,919 --> 00:08:00,309
What's interesting about that story, one of the things you wanted to talk about was latency and scale.

72
00:08:00,309 --> 00:08:10,710
That story illustrates how as you have a larger system and you either are scaling out for reasons of handling more traffic or other reasons, it also impacts your latency.

73
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:13,510
Absolutely.

74
00:08:13,510 --> 00:08:25,670
Like every time you expand a system, the kinds of places where you can incur latency, where you route traffic or you get it to the right place or your databases have higher load on it start to very quickly crop up.

75
00:08:25,670 --> 00:08:31,110
And often what happens is they kind of hit a tipping point at different parts of scale.

76
00:08:31,110 --> 00:08:41,269
So things will look fine for a really long time, then all of a sudden, bam, you'll expose a new source of latency and you're going to have to spend a bunch of time figuring that one out, optimizing it, and building the system for that growing scale.

77
00:08:41,269 --> 00:08:47,110
Do you advise or at your company do you institute latency regression testing?

78
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:57,269
The way that we have done it is primarily looking at analytics data from day-to-day.

79
00:08:57,269 --> 00:09:03,430
And so we'll have analytics on latency for every event that users experience with our application.

80
00:09:03,430 --> 00:09:11,350
And we will of course test things before we promote the deployments, but we'll basically do gradual rollouts of new infrastructure as we roll it out.

81
00:09:11,350 --> 00:09:14,070
And we will measure impact on latency there.

82
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:20,949
And so we can see on any given day, on any hour of any day, did the latency suddenly spike for any reason?

83
00:09:20,949 --> 00:09:27,350
We can see when we do new rollouts and new deployments, did it bring down the latency as we would expect it to?

84
00:09:27,350 --> 00:09:31,269
But we haven't done any specific kind of regression testing there.

85
00:09:31,269 --> 00:09:45,990
Maybe these systems we build now are so complex and impossible to really simulate in staging that you can't exactly test them before you roll them out, but you can detect when you have a problem quickly and resolve it.

86
00:09:45,990 --> 00:09:55,829
That's very much the approach that we take and that I think a lot of fastmoving companies in the AI space take right now because so many of these workloads are really stochastic.

87
00:09:55,839 --> 00:09:58,230
They're super random.

88
00:09:58,230 --> 00:10:09,590
different things happen on GPUs that are extremely non-predictable and non-deterministic and so making sure that you can immediately respond to any spike that's the most important thing for us.

89
00:10:09,590 --> 00:10:16,310
So hedge, we've been talking about latency for a bit, converging on the intersection of latency and AI.

90
00:10:16,310 --> 00:10:20,949
Let's talk about what AI is briefly.

91
00:10:20,949 --> 00:10:22,710
The way I observe this term,

92
00:10:22,710 --> 00:10:32,870
it's a bit of a moving target where it tends to overindex on what happened in the last 5 minutes, but we've had AI for decades now.

93
00:10:32,870 --> 00:10:40,790
If we're going to be talking about AI and latency, what domains of AIs are we talking about?

94
00:10:40,790 --> 00:10:44,870
I mean latency becomes important in any of these kinds of applications.

95
00:10:44,870 --> 00:10:54,389
So whether it's more classical techniques that are classifiers that are trained on certain kinds of data which are often used in recommendation systems

96
00:10:54,389 --> 00:11:01,750
and ads models or whether it's larger and larger ML workloads like large speech models or large language models, right?

97
00:11:01,750 --> 00:11:04,389
which are models that have billions of parameters.

98
00:11:04,399 --> 00:11:11,910
And in those kinds of cases, because they're running so much computation for any given request,

99
00:11:11,910 --> 00:11:22,470
that's the reason why latency has become such a hot topic in this kind of a community where before maybe we're running 20,000 floatingoint operations for getting one result back.

100
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:29,430
Now we might be running 10 billion or 100 billion floatingoint operations to get one result back.

101
00:11:29,430 --> 00:11:38,870
And as soon as you start to have to do that much computation, that's when optimizing these systems for a different type of latency constraint starts to become relevant.

102
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:46,630
Could you give an example of something using AI and what the latency expectations would be?

103
00:11:46,630 --> 00:11:49,590
Something that's not a large language model.

104
00:11:49,590 --> 00:12:03,030
So for not a large language model tools using AI include basically all the recommendation systems that people use for the Twitter algorithm, the Facebook algorithm for showing you different things.

105
00:12:03,030 --> 00:12:09,990
And in those kinds of cases, there's actually usually a cascade of different models that are running.

106
00:12:09,990 --> 00:12:17,750
Like first you'll use a model to retrieve a bunch of candidates of things that you might want to show the user.

107
00:12:17,750 --> 00:12:21,430
Then you actually rank those things.

108
00:12:21,430 --> 00:12:25,110
And so each stage of each model has a different latency requirement to like retrieve them.

109
00:12:25,110 --> 00:12:31,750
You might be operating on saying, hey, which of these like 10,000 posts are even potentially relevant to the user.

110
00:12:31,750 --> 00:12:36,470
And so that model has to be super super fast to be able to run on all those things.

111
00:12:36,470 --> 00:12:43,509
Then the next model might say, hey, of these 10 things, what's the thing that I show in the top place to the user?

112
00:12:43,509 --> 00:12:45,269
And with a lot of these systems,

113
00:12:45,269 --> 00:12:57,350
the way people get creative is you can show people the first page of results really fast and then you can have more time before the user scrolls to compute things that are going to show up on the second page.

114
00:12:57,350 --> 00:13:09,670
And so that's where like people really optimize for like first page load latency versus what you see after and can buy more time for running certain computations more efficiently.

115
00:13:09,670 --> 00:13:19,590
This reminds me of another question I had about latency where you can define it as the time from when you request until you start getting results.

116
00:13:19,590 --> 00:13:30,150
Is the thinking behind this last point if you can start showing the reader some results whether it's LLM chat or recommendations and we read pretty slow.

117
00:13:30,150 --> 00:13:40,470
So the reader's going to start reading and you have time now where you're ahead of the reader and you can fetch more results and the reader doesn't perceive it as lag. Is it something like that?

118
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:42,871
That's exactly right. Yeah.

119
00:13:42,871 --> 00:13:52,790
And it's taking advantage of the fact that we're engaging with the content and so we have plenty more time to run computation in the background while the user is actively engaged.

120
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:56,389
We discussed these findings.

121
00:13:56,389 --> 00:14:10,870
I recall a study that was covered in the news about Google if they introduced a 100 milliseconds delay in search that people almost stopped using Google for 3 months or something and it wasn't even a perceptible amount.

122
00:14:10,870 --> 00:14:15,350
It was something subconscious where the person feels like I'm having a bad experience.

123
00:14:15,350 --> 00:14:21,350
Yet with these LLMs, people will sit there for seconds waiting for an answer.

124
00:14:21,350 --> 00:14:28,629
Why is this tolerated when people seem to have such a short attention span in other domains?

125
00:14:28,639 --> 00:14:30,710
It's a great question.

126
00:14:30,710 --> 00:14:33,829
I think it comes back to two different things.

127
00:14:33,829 --> 00:14:44,550
So one like the immediate value that I get out of a LLM query is so high in so many cases like I'm immediately getting the final answer that I need.

128
00:14:44,550 --> 00:14:49,110
If you contrast it to Google, I've asked a question, I got 100 milliseconds.

129
00:14:49,110 --> 00:14:54,629
Now I have a bunch of search results that I now have to go click through to even see if I find the thing that I want to.

130
00:14:54,629 --> 00:15:01,189
And so there's so many steps involved there to get to the thing that I actually want to.

131
00:15:01,189 --> 00:15:10,790
Whereas with chat GPT like if it's going to take 2 seconds for me to get a magical answer that answers the world's most complex question that I previously didn't have a way to get an answer to, I'll wait.

132
00:15:10,790 --> 00:15:14,069
So that's like one part of it.

133
00:15:14,069 --> 00:15:16,710
And the other part is I think the user expectation that has been set.

134
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:25,189
So once people get used to a certain thing, it becomes very easy for them to continue working with that kind of model.

135
00:15:25,189 --> 00:15:36,710
But like I mentioned this earlier with the AI pause on voice mode, for example, on chat GPT where it used to pause for 3 or 4 seconds before it starts giving you an answer and they've reduced that to 500 milliseconds now.

136
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:47,590
After using the version where it responds like an actual person conversational partner, if it went back to having the long pause, I would probably stop using it very fast.

137
00:15:47,590 --> 00:15:49,749
And so I think there's a bit of both going on here.

138
00:15:49,759 --> 00:15:51,990
Yeah.

139
00:15:51,990 --> 00:15:59,670
I think you might be used to something and tolerate it, but you wouldn't get used to it if you weren't getting something out of it because you would simply stop using it.

140
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:02,389
Yeah.

141
00:16:02,389 --> 00:16:07,030
And like these systems are so magical in what they can provide a user so quickly.

142
00:16:07,030 --> 00:16:10,949
For me, for example, even if it gets half the things right in what it says in an answer, I'm still happy.

143
00:16:10,959 --> 00:16:20,470
Like, I've gotten 50% magical things that I could never have had before, and that's enough for me to come back to a tool like Chad GPT.

144
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:24,310
We've done some setup on latency and AI.

145
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:31,189
Let's delve into our core topic here, which is latency in AI applications.

146
00:16:31,199 --> 00:16:45,350
If you start for point of comparison dealing with a old school web application that's not hitting any kind of AI, what are some or all of the things that have to happen before a user gets a result?

147
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:47,509
Yeah.

148
00:16:47,509 --> 00:16:55,030
So in kind of an old school web application, the user will interact with a UI or passively they'll land on a page.

149
00:16:55,030 --> 00:16:56,949
Then a request will get sent to a backend.

150
00:16:56,949 --> 00:17:09,909
the backend will have to potentially authenticate a user, query some databases, generate a response and then send the response back to the client and then the client, their browser or whatever is showing it will actually have to render it.

151
00:17:09,909 --> 00:17:21,029
So you'll see different kind of chunks of time for sending the request, retrieving the response on the back end, getting it back to the user, and then finally showing it to them.

152
00:17:21,029 --> 00:17:23,350
I would imagine AI has to do pretty much the same things.

153
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:28,549
But can you say what might be different for an AI application?

154
00:17:28,559 --> 00:17:30,630
Yeah.

155
00:17:30,630 --> 00:17:40,310
So the difference for the AI application is this idea of you can't fetch the full response often all at once.

156
00:17:40,310 --> 00:17:45,270
So in a lot of traditional software, you're fetching the content that you want to show on a page.

157
00:17:45,270 --> 00:17:51,270
And so that's going to be some kind of query that gets a lot of this information and returns it back to the user.

158
00:17:51,270 --> 00:17:57,590
In a lot of AI applications, what people do is what's called auto reggressive generation.

159
00:17:57,590 --> 00:18:03,350
So if you think about chatgbt, it's generating one word at a time as you get an output.

160
00:18:03,350 --> 00:18:09,669
So a request gets sent for processing, then you have to basically queue up all the stuff to get the first word of the response.

161
00:18:09,669 --> 00:18:12,549
Then you have to feed that back into the model to get the next word.

162
00:18:12,549 --> 00:18:16,310
then you have to feed that back in to get the next word and so on.

163
00:18:16,310 --> 00:18:20,470
So in a lot of more traditional software, there's a lot less of this sequential acting.

164
00:18:20,470 --> 00:18:21,990
There might be three or four steps.

165
00:18:21,990 --> 00:18:27,270
Maybe you retrieve something from a database, then you figure out, okay, how does that mean that I should update the UI?

166
00:18:27,270 --> 00:18:30,549
But they're not heavy computations and they're usually discrete steps, right?

167
00:18:30,549 --> 00:18:32,950
You're following a linear code path.

168
00:18:32,950 --> 00:18:41,190
Here you're often just following a loop where you're doing a loop of decoding something and then eventually giving it back to the user.

169
00:18:41,190 --> 00:18:46,150
I'd say the other maybe part that I'd add there is GPU computation.

170
00:18:46,150 --> 00:18:54,230
So in most traditional software, most of the requests are handled by a CPU because you have a server somewhere that can do the database operations and return things.

171
00:18:54,240 --> 00:19:02,470
Whereas for a lot of machine learning workloads, you're going to have to have a GPU that's actually processing and providing a result.

172
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:05,110
That opens up a lot of implications.

173
00:19:05,110 --> 00:19:12,150
The first thing I can think of is obviously you have some of the response before you have all of the response.

174
00:19:12,150 --> 00:19:20,710
So you could start sending data back to the user before you have all of it which is generally not the case in old school web apps.

175
00:19:20,720 --> 00:19:29,669
Exactly. Now we talk a lot also in podcast about streaming applications where data is arriving.

176
00:19:29,669 --> 00:19:31,350
Does this make AI more like a streaming application?

177
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:33,590
It does in a lot of ways.

178
00:19:33,590 --> 00:19:40,870
streaming applications like audio chat, video chat, and all of these kinds of things have to handle a lot of challenges like this.

179
00:19:40,870 --> 00:19:43,430
There's a couple differences, I think.

180
00:19:43,430 --> 00:19:52,150
One is a lot of streaming applications and traditional software that stream audio and video, they're handling really, really big data requests when you're streaming.

181
00:19:52,150 --> 00:19:57,110
And that's the thing that makes it super intensive.

182
00:19:57,110 --> 00:20:01,110
Whereas in a lot of these AI workloads, the streaming itself, it's not much data.

183
00:20:01,110 --> 00:20:02,630
you send back a text response.

184
00:20:02,630 --> 00:20:04,950
It's like there's not that much there.

185
00:20:04,950 --> 00:20:11,110
But the computation on the server is the thing that's super stochastic, super random, and can take a really long time.

186
00:20:11,110 --> 00:20:13,190
And that's what you're trying to hide for the user.

187
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:16,070
I observe LLM apps.

188
00:20:16,070 --> 00:20:19,110
They seem to send me back roughly paragraphs sized chunks.

189
00:20:19,120 --> 00:20:21,430
You could send one word at a time.

190
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:25,350
What's the thinking?

191
00:20:25,350 --> 00:20:28,950
Is it n words or in batches of certain number of milliseconds?

192
00:20:28,960 --> 00:20:34,310
It comes back to actually the user experience.

193
00:20:34,310 --> 00:20:38,470
So the reason how people optimize latency is so that it feels good to the user.

194
00:20:38,470 --> 00:20:41,110
That's why people send back partial responses.

195
00:20:41,110 --> 00:20:48,630
As a user, whenever I've prototyped something for myself where I stream back one word at a time,

196
00:20:48,630 --> 00:20:56,070
it's completely impossible to read the output because it's just my screen is changing so fast that I'm actually losing track and not having a good time.

197
00:20:56,080 --> 00:21:01,029
And so that's part of the reason why people stream it in that way.

198
00:21:01,029 --> 00:21:03,909
And people have played around actually with a lot of different streaming patterns.

199
00:21:03,909 --> 00:21:12,390
Like Gemini used to fade in one line of text at a time and like they're not coming in faded in that way off the server.

200
00:21:12,390 --> 00:21:16,390
That's primarily a how do you display it to the user so they stay engaged.

201
00:21:16,390 --> 00:21:17,909
I do think they moved away from that though.

202
00:21:17,919 --> 00:21:22,230
I have queued up some questions more about managing latency.

203
00:21:22,230 --> 00:21:27,750
But an obvious question I have here is how much of a response does the user want from an LLM?

204
00:21:27,760 --> 00:21:40,710
I've noticed using different models that they seem to have a different view on how long the response are where certain models are consistently giving me more bigger responses.

205
00:21:40,710 --> 00:21:46,710
Now an obvious way to control latency would be to send the user a smaller response.

206
00:21:46,710 --> 00:21:49,430
How do you approach that tradeoff?

207
00:21:49,430 --> 00:21:54,230
Are you curious how we approach it at Whisper or how the industry approaches it for different things?

208
00:21:54,240 --> 00:21:56,470
Well, actually, if it's different, yeah, both.

209
00:21:56,480 --> 00:22:01,669
So, for us, we are a voice dictation application.

210
00:22:01,669 --> 00:22:10,070
You speak to your computer and it will enter the text of what you said, clean it up for you so that you can actually use it for emails, Slack messages, and so on.

211
00:22:10,070 --> 00:22:18,230
For that kind of application, people don't want it to go off the rails and start like expanding on your behalf and deciding what you want to say on your behalf.

212
00:22:18,230 --> 00:22:25,350
that's actually very much like I want the user to experience that they get to choose what shows up in the system.

213
00:22:25,350 --> 00:22:34,789
And so we're really careful to make sure that the responses that we produce because we run an LLM to post-process things are very similar size to the input.

214
00:22:34,789 --> 00:22:43,510
It's actually a very strict requirement that we have now on other applications where it's more of a question answering style thing

215
00:22:43,510 --> 00:22:53,830
or even for us when we start going voice to action and we're going to generate longer and longer responses that's when this comes back to user preference.

216
00:22:53,830 --> 00:23:02,870
So the most important thing is like actually all of these different applications and services will adapt to what people want and what people seem to like.

217
00:23:02,870 --> 00:23:07,909
And I think the reason why they all produce different responses is because they've all been designed with a different goal in mind.

218
00:23:07,909 --> 00:23:21,270
For example, Chat GPT is largely like a consumer application built with a lot of former Meta employees and so they optimize probably for engagement and time spent in the application.

219
00:23:21,270 --> 00:23:28,070
I'm speculating there, but I think that's part of the reason why it tends to produce longer responses.

220
00:23:28,070 --> 00:23:36,710
Claude and Anthropics models have been optimized for coding and tasks where you're generating for a very precise task, which is why in my experience, they've been more concise.

221
00:23:36,720 --> 00:23:47,430
But I've noticed in my own use, if I hit expand for more details on one answer, then subsequent answers all start to get longer and longer from that same service.

222
00:23:47,430 --> 00:23:51,190
And so that's about adapting to, hey, what does this user want in this moment?

223
00:23:51,190 --> 00:23:55,990
How can I make sure I give them what they as an individual are looking for?

224
00:23:55,990 --> 00:24:04,390
Your response about whisper makes it reminds me of a time I had a job condensing interviews from this podcast into written.

225
00:24:04,390 --> 00:24:16,870
They were called transcripts but I was approaching it to trying to preserve information and I found that people when they speak are highly verbose use about two and a half times more words than written English.

226
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:28,470
Does your application do any condensation where you're trying to preserve the meaning and eliminate redundancy which I also found the redundancy is much more obvious when you read it.

227
00:24:28,480 --> 00:24:30,950
Yeah, for sure.

228
00:24:30,950 --> 00:24:33,029
What we found is there's two types of things that people want.

229
00:24:33,029 --> 00:24:36,710
So one is when people are speaking short messages.

230
00:24:36,710 --> 00:24:40,149
Suppose I'm speaking into Slack or text message.

231
00:24:40,149 --> 00:24:51,110
There actually a lot of my filler words things like like though and stuff like that are super important for me to preserve my tone because I want to be super soft, super casual in those kinds of places.

232
00:24:51,110 --> 00:24:56,950
But where this kind of condensing and rewriting really matters is like people will speak to their email for a minute.

233
00:24:56,960 --> 00:25:01,590
Like nobody can write a precisely spoken email for a whole minute.

234
00:25:01,590 --> 00:25:03,830
That's just a really hard task to be able to do.

235
00:25:03,830 --> 00:25:07,991
And so now we do basically some light editing. We'll break it into paragraphs.

236
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:09,830
We'll structure it.

237
00:25:09,830 --> 00:25:11,350
We'll make it look a little bit better for you.

238
00:25:11,350 --> 00:25:21,350
We're about to launch a mode where it does some kind of heavier rewriting for you where you should still be able to scan the output so you know that we haven't deleted any meaning from what you said,

239
00:25:21,350 --> 00:25:25,830
but the person receiving it can read it really, really easily.

240
00:25:25,830 --> 00:25:27,669
And different people will want different things there.

241
00:25:27,669 --> 00:25:30,310
Some people are super precise in how they speak.

242
00:25:30,320 --> 00:25:33,750
Some people just want to speak how they would speak to a friend.

243
00:25:33,750 --> 00:25:39,430
and have it so that the system understands them and cleans up all of the extra redundancy and makes it really really intelligible.

244
00:25:39,440 --> 00:25:49,830
We may have covered this to some extent but I think you would have some new things to say about what are specific challenges in engineering

245
00:25:49,830 --> 00:25:54,710
for low latency around AI that we haven't already covered.

246
00:25:54,720 --> 00:26:02,711
So I think the biggest one is this trade-off between latency and throughput. So let's put it this way.

247
00:26:02,711 --> 00:26:09,110
So when people submit requests to chat GPT, right, they're asking a question.

248
00:26:09,110 --> 00:26:12,390
The server can choose one of two things.

249
00:26:12,390 --> 00:26:21,830
It can process my request immediately, or it can wait for my request and your request to show up and then send them to the GPU at the same time.

250
00:26:21,830 --> 00:26:23,590
And there's actually a really big trade-off here.

251
00:26:23,590 --> 00:26:27,750
So if you process my request immediately, I'll get my response really quickly.

252
00:26:27,750 --> 00:26:38,710
But if I wait for yours, then often because I'm running on a GPU, I can run those requests in parallel and make really good use of my hardware.

253
00:26:38,710 --> 00:26:44,470
And so I could serve twice as many requests on the same amount of compute capacity.

254
00:26:44,470 --> 00:26:55,830
And so there becomes this really big trade-off between, hey, how do we optimize the latency and how do we optimize serving as many users as we can with the same number of GPUs?

255
00:26:55,830 --> 00:27:01,269
And if you've ever heard about the GPU shortage or if you've ever seen Nvidia stock go up,

256
00:27:01,269 --> 00:27:09,430
you know that people are actually really clinging to how much juice can they get out of every GPU.

257
00:27:09,430 --> 00:27:11,190
How can you actually get free users to be able to use more requests?

258
00:27:11,190 --> 00:27:14,233
And so this trade-off is like very specific

259
00:27:14,243 --> 00:27:15,110
[laughter]

260
00:27:15,120 --> 00:27:23,110
to a lot of AI workloads and depends a lot on the application that you're building.

261
00:27:23,110 --> 00:27:26,470
Are there any other trade-offs possibly involving accuracy?

262
00:27:26,480 --> 00:27:30,789
For sure.

263
00:27:30,789 --> 00:27:33,190
So, in general, bigger models will give you better answers.

264
00:27:33,190 --> 00:27:35,269
Smaller models will give worse answers.

265
00:27:35,269 --> 00:27:41,350
It's like a rough rule of thumb, and bigger models are slower, smaller models are really fast.

266
00:27:41,350 --> 00:27:45,750
You can get really tiny models that run on, for example, a MacBook's Apple Silicon chip.

267
00:27:45,750 --> 00:27:50,230
For example, open source models like Llama 8B.

268
00:27:50,230 --> 00:27:59,430
you can quantize it and put it on your device and it will produce super fast responses but they won't be very good

269
00:27:59,430 --> 00:28:13,350
and so the challenge is figuring out for any given application for any given request how do I route that request to a model with the right level of complexity so if you're building for example an enduserfacing application some things

270
00:28:13,350 --> 00:28:22,950
that a user might do might require a really complicated model like maybe you're building a medical health app and for example processing certain data requires the latest

271
00:28:22,950 --> 00:28:29,990
and greatest GPT 5.1 models and that's going to be really slow but then other questions or other things that a user might be doing

272
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:38,710
where you're just trying to figure out basic things like is this person happy sad or what's their tone right now that might be able

273
00:28:38,710 --> 00:28:52,389
to get by with a much smaller model and so for both users and developers this becomes a really important consideration what size model you're deploying And do you use any of these kinds of new techniques to take the same models and make them faster?

274
00:28:52,399 --> 00:29:06,230
And I know you're interested in some of these topics with respect to distillation, quantization, using certain compute efficient architectures, ones that work faster for training versus faster at inference time.

275
00:29:06,230 --> 00:29:14,549
There's like all sorts of knobs that you can tune that will allow you to serve different quality outputs to users in different ways.

276
00:29:14,559 --> 00:29:16,389
Yeah.

277
00:29:16,389 --> 00:29:18,230
And we could talk about some of those things.

278
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:29,909
Let me ask so the AI it's much more expensive to run at least the larger AIs compared to conventional web applications.

279
00:29:29,909 --> 00:29:40,470
I remember seeing some data that AI search compared to a Google index search I don't remember 10 times more expensive multiples more easily.

280
00:29:40,480 --> 00:29:51,830
All these trade-offs you're talking about are really come down to resource tradeoffs which in the end you could give the customer whatever they want if they are willing to cover your costs.

281
00:29:51,840 --> 00:29:56,070
How do you approach making these trade-offs in a real application?

282
00:29:56,080 --> 00:30:09,110
Well, one I would say almost if we had infinite GPUs, but OpenAI and a lot of the biggest language model providers just don't even have enough GPUs to service the requests.

283
00:30:09,110 --> 00:30:14,710
like if they did they might actually be able to serve people more large model requests.

284
00:30:14,710 --> 00:30:20,789
Now to your kind of other question of like hey suppose it is under the practical resource constraint trade-off space

285
00:30:20,799 --> 00:30:31,029
which is you know the vast majority of cases where you're not running up against literally somebody does not have enough GPUs in a data center right now to handle the requests that a user has.

286
00:30:31,039 --> 00:30:33,430
It's very case by case.

287
00:30:33,430 --> 00:30:39,750
So, for example, for us, we're building voice dictation where the user expects a nearly instantaneous response, right?

288
00:30:39,750 --> 00:30:41,909
It's an interactive voice application,

289
00:30:41,909 --> 00:30:52,870
but if the user has to go back and fix up their transcript or clean up a bunch of stuff afterwards or edit a mistake in the speech recognition, that experience is no longer better than typing.

290
00:30:52,870 --> 00:31:01,350
And so, we have basically evaluated models at different sizes where we say we have a latency budget.

291
00:31:01,350 --> 00:31:07,029
Our LLM for 50 words or 100 words can only take 250 milliseconds to process.

292
00:31:07,029 --> 00:31:19,510
And then we're gonna try models of various classes in various families to see what is the most accurate one that we're able to train that gets the accuracy we want at the latency budget that we want.

293
00:31:19,510 --> 00:31:23,750
And we found that going too small or quantizing models will make it worse.

294
00:31:23,750 --> 00:31:29,269
We found that going bigger and bigger at some points might not even help or might exceed our latency threshold.

295
00:31:29,269 --> 00:31:31,350
So that's how we've done it for our application.

296
00:31:31,350 --> 00:31:40,470
A lot of other places that are doing things that are more like agentbased where the model is deciding what tool to use.

297
00:31:40,470 --> 00:31:44,149
You can even have a model make the decision about how to route the request.

298
00:31:44,149 --> 00:31:46,310
So you could have a request get sent somewhere.

299
00:31:46,320 --> 00:31:52,789
Some medium or big model might decide, hey, is this a easy, hard, medium question?

300
00:31:52,789 --> 00:31:54,549
and send it off to the right thing.

301
00:31:54,549 --> 00:32:03,509
After that, you just now mentioned latency budgets, which is something I have on my list to talk to you about. Can you explain what that is?

302
00:32:03,519 --> 00:32:14,230
Yeah, so a latency budget is basically us saying this is the maximum allowable time that a user can experience for a request.

303
00:32:14,230 --> 00:32:24,070
And we can set the budget either for the average request or for the extreme like 99th percentile worst request.

304
00:32:24,070 --> 00:32:34,230
And so we do this as a kind of product side design the constraint around the engineering and then try all possible solutions that we can to meet that constraint.

305
00:32:34,230 --> 00:32:44,549
Is this constraint enforced on each and every request as it goes through the system or is it more of a goal that you're trying to meet on an average or P99 basis?

306
00:32:44,559 --> 00:32:50,470
We do it as a soft target for something that we want to meet on an average or a P99 basis.

307
00:32:50,470 --> 00:32:54,870
So we'll have a different target for our average latency, a different target for our P99 latency.

308
00:32:54,880 --> 00:33:05,669
And then when we evaluate new things we want to build, we see, hey, is this going to even be practical to meet that P50,

309
00:33:05,669 --> 00:33:14,630
P99 latency target because in practice, every request is so different to these AI workloads that it's hard to guarantee that every single thing will complete under a very fixed threshold.

310
00:33:14,640 --> 00:33:20,870
We talked a little while ago about all the things that have to happen for the user to get a completed request.

311
00:33:20,870 --> 00:33:23,750
You look at the budget.

312
00:33:23,750 --> 00:33:35,669
Are you using this as a planning tool where you say, "Okay, DNS takes two milliseconds and database takes three or how do you allocate the budget among the different steps?"

313
00:33:35,679 --> 00:33:42,149
The way that we've always approached it is we've kind of started with the overall target.

314
00:33:42,149 --> 00:33:44,310
Then we figure out, hey, what's the breakdown right now?

315
00:33:44,310 --> 00:33:46,389
How much is each step taking right now?

316
00:33:46,389 --> 00:33:48,149
And how much could each of those be optimized?

317
00:33:48,159 --> 00:33:56,149
So at the end of the day, all I care about is the end user's experience, right?

318
00:33:56,149 --> 00:34:03,350
And then based on how much time each chunk of it taking, we can say, okay, this chunk can be optimized further, so we can make more time for this other chunk, right?

319
00:34:03,350 --> 00:34:09,109
Maybe if we make our network operations 100 milliseconds faster, now we get 100 milliseconds back to run a bigger LLM.

320
00:34:09,119 --> 00:34:10,790
That would be nice, wouldn't it?

321
00:34:10,790 --> 00:34:12,869
And so that's how we start.

322
00:34:12,869 --> 00:34:14,950
We always set the constraint on the enduser experience.

323
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:26,950
And then when we're doing engineering, we're trying to figure out, okay, right now based on all the chunks of the pipeline, we only have this much time for this set of operations and then we make it happen.

324
00:34:26,960 --> 00:34:38,149
Can you tell a story about a time where you said we need to get this particular step to come in a 100 milliseconds slower and what you had to do to make that happen?

325
00:34:38,159 --> 00:34:45,510
So a huge one for us was with respect to deploying our LLMs, right?

326
00:34:45,510 --> 00:34:50,550
So, our LLM and other LLMs for things like coding agents

327
00:34:50,550 --> 00:35:02,150
where they're editing the output of what somebody has said require essentially taking in all the words and outputting something that looks very similar but edits the output.

328
00:35:02,150 --> 00:35:07,750
And so that whole step was taking a really long time initially.

329
00:35:07,750 --> 00:35:15,589
But there's kind of one category of techniques that people use across a lot of ML workloads called speculative decoding where essentially what you're doing is you're going

330
00:35:15,589 --> 00:35:23,589
to say, "Hey, I'm going to guess a bunch of things about what the output should be and just verify my guess more or less."

331
00:35:23,599 --> 00:35:33,349
So, for example, if you're in like a coding tool and you're editing a line or you're going to edit several lines, you can actually guess that like,

332
00:35:33,349 --> 00:35:40,390
hey, this line's going to stay the same and just reoutput the same line that was already there so that you only have to output the lines that are going to get changed.

333
00:35:40,400 --> 00:35:53,670
And similarly for us, right, if you're dictating and we're going to remove a bunch of filler words, you can guess that, hey, if if the sentence is looking fine, it's probably going to be the same words up to a certain point before we have to start changing things.

334
00:35:53,670 --> 00:35:57,910
And so this is one of those kinds of techniques where once we roll that out,

335
00:35:57,910 --> 00:36:07,829
we're actually able to bring down the latency for LLMs by over half in a lot of our cases, sometimes even more.

336
00:36:07,829 --> 00:36:15,110
And that allowed us actually budget for running even more models as part of our workflow. Right?

337
00:36:15,110 --> 00:36:22,230
By doing that, we were able to say, okay, how can we make the results even better by running a third model and a fourth model to do things that are even more useful for the user?

338
00:36:22,240 --> 00:36:36,230
I've worked more in enterprise software and I can't say that companies don't care at all about latency, but the approach is generally more building features that create some value within certain workflows.

339
00:36:36,230 --> 00:36:46,390
And then you'll maybe look at latency and if you have certain features that are way out of line, you try to figure out what's going on and bring them back to the maybe the average.

340
00:36:46,390 --> 00:36:57,191
But from this conversation, it sounds like there are problem domains where latency is a key part of the product and you just wouldn't have a product that would be successful without latency. So it can't be an afterthought.

341
00:36:57,200 --> 00:37:08,470
is first is that an accurate description of either your business domain or some related domains 100%.

342
00:37:08,470 --> 00:37:16,550
I think anything that's a user interactive sticky habit that's being used directly by individuals and not bought by a company has this kind of attribute to it.

343
00:37:16,550 --> 00:37:27,190
So that's like superhuman mail, email clients, like web browsers, notepads, editing applications, things like Slack or messaging applications,

344
00:37:27,190 --> 00:37:32,710
anything that's individual facing, all those things have extremely tight latency requirements.

345
00:37:32,720 --> 00:37:42,550
You could say if you're the user of enterprise software, you're an employee of a company which has bought this software for you to use as part of your job.

346
00:37:42,550 --> 00:37:49,510
And so you're not going to say, "Hey, I'm going to switch to this other enterprise software." So you just have to put up with it.

347
00:37:49,510 --> 00:37:57,430
But may also be true that companies don't want their employees to get bored, distracted, attention wander, and they want their employees to be productive.

348
00:37:57,430 --> 00:38:04,069
So do you have any thoughts on how important latency is even when you have a somewhat captive user base?

349
00:38:04,079 --> 00:38:12,550
The best consumer products often can also be some of the best enterprise products.

350
00:38:12,550 --> 00:38:24,550
like we all still use things like iPhones or Android phones for our work applications and they're also individual products and so companies that do this incredibly well can capture a lot of the market on both sides.

351
00:38:24,550 --> 00:38:35,750
I think the the real trade-off comes from how many features can you ship quickly if you're also trying to optimize the latency and performance of every feature.

352
00:38:35,750 --> 00:38:39,349
And so in a lot of enterprise cases,

353
00:38:39,349 --> 00:38:48,950
there's actual value unlocked by having a complex new workflow feature that integrates in five different data sources and lets you do something new with it, right?

354
00:38:48,950 --> 00:38:52,710
And maybe only 5% of the users at the company are going to use that specific feature.

355
00:38:52,710 --> 00:39:04,390
And so in those kinds of cases, the reason why a lot of enterprise software focuses less on this by default is you can't do that and also ship all the features as quickly.

356
00:39:04,390 --> 00:39:05,910
That's the real reason for the trade-off.

357
00:39:05,920 --> 00:39:08,550
Yeah, that makes sense.

358
00:39:08,550 --> 00:39:13,670
Now, we've covered some of the techniques for lowering low latency engineering in AI.

359
00:39:13,680 --> 00:39:17,910
You talked about choosing the right model.

360
00:39:17,910 --> 00:39:23,030
you went by pretty quickly reducing quantization, distillation, pruning.

361
00:39:23,030 --> 00:39:24,630
So let's maybe dive into those.

362
00:39:24,630 --> 00:39:27,430
We could start with quant reducing quantization.

363
00:39:27,440 --> 00:39:29,990
Yeah.

364
00:39:29,990 --> 00:39:40,550
So, one of the big techniques that people use to actually make workloads really fast is instead of running all of your operations in 32-bit floatingoint arithmetic,

365
00:39:40,550 --> 00:39:47,589
which is pretty slow, you could run it in 16- bit floatingoint arithmetic or even better, you could run it in 8bit integer arithmetic.

366
00:39:47,589 --> 00:39:58,710
Integer arithmetic is way way way faster to run on compute than floatingoint arithmetic because you don't have to kind of carry over any exponents or do any complicated computations.

367
00:39:58,710 --> 00:40:02,470
The problem comes with the fact that a lot of these models were trained using floatingoint arithmetic.

368
00:40:02,480 --> 00:40:12,310
So now when you want to port them over to using integer arithmetic to serve them really fast, the accuracy starts to go down because they're doing something slightly different.

369
00:40:12,310 --> 00:40:25,670
And so people do a lot of work basically to train models or fine-tune models in a way that's essentially quantization aware that's aware of the fact that in the future all the operations are going to be redone as integer operations.

370
00:40:25,670 --> 00:40:33,829
that then lets you deploy these kinds of quantized models on edge devices or on servers as well and still get really good results.

371
00:40:33,839 --> 00:40:38,710
Can you give an example of a problem domain where that works?

372
00:40:38,720 --> 00:40:49,190
I actually suspect almost all of the large language models that are deployed right now are operating on quantized arithmetic at inference time

373
00:40:49,190 --> 00:40:57,190
because like you can get four times compared to 16- bit integer but significantly more than four times compared to 16- bit floating point

374
00:40:57,190 --> 00:41:05,190
and so so much of the inference for these models is actually optimized for running an integer inference and like you'll see for example plenty of posts

375
00:41:05,190 --> 00:41:19,109
where Elon talks about how the fact that these models are trained in floating point itself is an issue and he's trying to push for a lot of the XAI work to train in integer arithmetic as well because then things can be even faster.

376
00:41:19,119 --> 00:41:26,069
Do you know if some of the biometric IDs that run on our phone fit that category?

377
00:41:26,079 --> 00:41:35,030
I actually don't know too much about how those are implemented or if they're using some of these really big workloads.

378
00:41:35,030 --> 00:41:40,790
quantization I've mostly seen people use for models that exceed you know 100 million parameters where you're

379
00:41:40,800 --> 00:41:52,390
now trying to bring it back to the device to run it but I don't think the biometric ID models are actually really really big models I think they're actually tuned in a different way for precision

380
00:41:52,390 --> 00:42:05,430
and recall distillation that's the next thing on my list how can that be used to impact latency so distillation is a really huge technique and it's used extremely widely.

381
00:42:05,430 --> 00:42:08,069
And so the idea here is small models are less smart than big models.

382
00:42:08,079 --> 00:42:13,190
Small models have less capacity to learn and understand things.

383
00:42:13,190 --> 00:42:19,589
However, if you're telling a small model to do something specific, it can learn how to do that very specific thing.

384
00:42:19,589 --> 00:42:27,750
And one of the ways that somebody can teach a small model to learn how to do that very specific thing is to ask a large model to teach it essentially.

385
00:42:27,750 --> 00:42:34,309
And the way that you get a large model to teach it is maybe you have a million examples of the task that you want to do.

386
00:42:34,309 --> 00:42:39,990
And you take those million examples, you have the large model try and perform the task on all million examples.

387
00:42:39,990 --> 00:42:41,990
And you get the answers that it produces.

388
00:42:41,990 --> 00:42:49,750
And you use that as the data to say, hey, let me now train the small model to mimic what the big model would have done.

389
00:42:49,750 --> 00:42:56,309
And now all of a sudden, the small model might not have been able to do the task to begin with because it doesn't have the capacity to learn everything on its own.

390
00:42:56,319 --> 00:43:08,470
But now that it's seen lots and lots of examples of how to do that, it can do that one thing really well, often just as well as the big model and probably not much else anymore at that point.

391
00:43:08,470 --> 00:43:11,750
And this technique is super powerful. It's used kind of across the board.

392
00:43:11,750 --> 00:43:20,390
So the way that I just described it was how it's used in large language models where people might distill a very large language model into a small language model.

393
00:43:20,390 --> 00:43:29,589
But it's also used across the board for example for computer vision models or speech models or things of that sort where you can get or even recommener systems.

394
00:43:29,589 --> 00:43:36,150
You can get really strong properties out of small models by training a much larger model first offline.

395
00:43:36,160 --> 00:43:46,230
Given a large language model, what's a good example of a use case where you want a small language model that's good at one particular thing?

396
00:43:46,230 --> 00:43:51,190
Basically, anything domain specific is a good example of this.

397
00:43:51,190 --> 00:44:00,790
So, let's take your example that you mentioned where you would often have recordings of interviews that you'd done where you were trying to get them to be shorter.

398
00:44:00,800 --> 00:44:06,470
And suppose you're trying to do that at scale, right, for lots and lots of things.

399
00:44:06,470 --> 00:44:13,510
If you sent every single one of those requests to GPT 5.1, it might be really slow, might take forever, might cost you a lot of money.

400
00:44:13,510 --> 00:44:20,950
And as a company, if you're doing that, or as an individual, if you're doing that, if you could turn that into a task that could be accomplished by a small model,

401
00:44:20,950 --> 00:44:27,829
it solves for a lot of the resource constraint trade-offs that you mentioned and that we were talking about earlier, right?

402
00:44:27,829 --> 00:44:34,950
Because now all you have to do is first either online or offline, you can generate all this data, then you train the small model once,

403
00:44:34,950 --> 00:44:41,910
and now every subsequent thing that you do is super fast, super cheap, and accomplishes the same result.

404
00:44:41,910 --> 00:44:47,990
And so like all sorts of domain specific things and domain specific applications, legal AI will have a lot of these kinds of things.

405
00:44:47,990 --> 00:44:57,109
A lot of medical AI tools will do this because it lets you serve a lot more requests at low latency, at low cost, still with quality.

406
00:44:57,109 --> 00:44:59,990
The other one is also for ondevice inference.

407
00:44:59,990 --> 00:45:07,270
So like if you want to bring something to work on your phone or on your laptop and you want

408
00:45:07,270 --> 00:45:18,390
to have a really highowered AI workload on that, sometimes the only way to get that to work on these really really you know small computers that exist on on a phone is to actually use this kind of a technique.

409
00:45:18,400 --> 00:45:29,349
I would love to have chat GPT on my laptop but what's a example of a limited scope application that's very useful to be able to run offline?

410
00:45:29,349 --> 00:45:36,150
A good example of this, and I don't know if Grammarly does this, but like grammar checkers for example,

411
00:45:36,150 --> 00:45:40,470
or autocorrect built into your keyboard or things like this where if you have a good language model,

412
00:45:40,470 --> 00:45:51,671
it should be able to accomplish this task really well and it just has to run fast and on my device because I can't send every single one of those requests to a server. So that's one example.

413
00:45:51,671 --> 00:45:53,910
A lot of vision models, this is really important.

414
00:45:53,920 --> 00:45:59,829
So vision models will often want to run on my phone or on my device.

415
00:45:59,829 --> 00:46:06,710
If for example I'm looking through a camera app and I want to tag objects that I'm looking at or if Tesla is driving a car,

416
00:46:06,720 --> 00:46:15,990
the amount of compute they can actually run on the device in the car is going to be lower than what they can train when they're training their models with their servers.

417
00:46:15,990 --> 00:46:29,349
And so, you know, any of these kinds of things where you don't want to be reliant on a network for being able to make a request and then you don't have to run servers as a developer yourself.

418
00:46:29,349 --> 00:46:31,910
You can have everything run on the user's device.

419
00:46:31,910 --> 00:46:37,030
And then for privacy sensitive applications, it's kind of the final core one where it's hey, no data will leave the device.

420
00:46:37,030 --> 00:46:40,631
It runs right there. And that's super useful.

421
00:46:40,631 --> 00:46:47,430
AI are LLMs in particular are different in the amount of randomness they introduce into the response.

422
00:46:47,430 --> 00:46:49,990
What are some techniques for managing that?

423
00:46:49,990 --> 00:47:00,069
This is one of the hardest parts about serving a lot of these applications and one of the ways in which that occurs is the kind of classic problem of hallucinations in AI.

424
00:47:00,079 --> 00:47:11,270
So maybe you ask a question and then all of a sudden the model goes off in a crazy loop and then starts to even do bad things like repeat the same sentence over

425
00:47:11,270 --> 00:47:19,990
and over and over again in an answer and then the request just never stops, never terminates and keeps consuming resources, never giving the user what they want.

426
00:47:19,990 --> 00:47:25,109
There's a bunch of different pathologies like this that can show up when serving AI applications.

427
00:47:25,119 --> 00:47:30,390
And there's two or three important things to do to handle this.

428
00:47:30,390 --> 00:47:32,950
So the first is like safeguards.

429
00:47:32,950 --> 00:47:38,710
So if you know how long an answer should be, that at least lets you cut off how bad of an output you can generate.

430
00:47:38,710 --> 00:47:40,790
So that's one thing that's really helpful.

431
00:47:40,790 --> 00:47:47,510
The second thing is catching it as the model is producing it.

432
00:47:47,510 --> 00:47:55,750
So one thing that can be done is like if the model starts hallucinating going off the rails start answering a different question from what it was asked

433
00:47:55,750 --> 00:48:10,230
that can be caught part way because the output's being partially produced and heristic algorithms can be used to catch that other LLM could be used to catch that all of these kinds of things allow for handling those kinds of problems and so you know this becomes really important because it's like even

434
00:48:10,240 --> 00:48:18,950
with normal questions that a user might ask sometimes these systems just go off the rails And as a developer,

435
00:48:18,950 --> 00:48:24,710
it's important that you're the one responsible for making sure the system doesn't go off the rails when a user is asking a normal question,

436
00:48:24,720 --> 00:48:32,630
let alone for the kind of handful of users who will try to break your system and give it hard things that will cause those kinds of problems to occur.

437
00:48:32,640 --> 00:48:38,390
Now, in the last few minutes, I want to talk about the issues of scale.

438
00:48:38,390 --> 00:48:44,230
How does latency degrade or become increasingly challenging at scale?

439
00:48:44,230 --> 00:48:46,549
So scale has a tremendous amount of impact on latency

440
00:48:46,559 --> 00:48:59,270
and I think a lot of it has to do with how GPU requests tend to actually get processed the more and more a load is on the GPU.

441
00:48:59,270 --> 00:49:01,030
So GPUs work by parallelizing lots of operations.

442
00:49:01,030 --> 00:49:03,430
They run lots of computations in parallel.

443
00:49:03,430 --> 00:49:11,030
But if two people, for example, submit a request at the same time, and mine has a, you know, 200word question with a 10-word answer,

444
00:49:11,040 --> 00:49:23,750
and yours has a 10-word question with a 200word answer, and the GPU tries to process them simultaneously, it now all of a sudden has to do two slightly different things for each of us.

445
00:49:23,750 --> 00:49:28,630
And so the way in which it can optimize the parallel computation changes, right?

446
00:49:28,630 --> 00:49:38,549
And so there's a lot of things that occur as systems and requests start to scale where more and more different types of requests get batched.

447
00:49:38,549 --> 00:49:43,349
And on the flip side, you have more opportunity to optimize it, right?

448
00:49:43,349 --> 00:49:46,390
So I can try as somebody working on infrastructure to optimize it.

449
00:49:46,390 --> 00:49:48,870
So similar kinds of requests go to the same server, right?

450
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:56,230
And so that gives me opportunities for optimizing latency in a way that wasn't possible when there wasn't scale.

451
00:49:56,230 --> 00:50:00,309
And so scale both helps and hurts when it comes to latency.

452
00:50:00,309 --> 00:50:10,710
The place where it'll probably hurt the most is if you ever run out of resources and then now you're dealing with higher and higher load on the same set of machines and doing your best to make sure that you can actually service everybody.

453
00:50:10,720 --> 00:50:20,069
And how much of this behaviors that are more um unique to GPUs do you know going into it?

454
00:50:20,069 --> 00:50:27,030
And how much have you discovered with observability and digging into things that were not behaving as you expected?

455
00:50:27,040 --> 00:50:29,829
I'd probably say 50/50.

456
00:50:29,829 --> 00:50:36,710
Like there's a bunch of these kinds of problems that we know going in where we haven't solved them yet.

457
00:50:36,710 --> 00:50:40,150
And we know that at the next batch of scale it's going to be important to solve them.

458
00:50:40,150 --> 00:50:47,430
And there's a bunch of things that come up that are extremely hard to predict because sometimes one flag

459
00:50:47,430 --> 00:50:58,470
that you set for an NVIDIA CUDA kernel is the kind of thing that changes the performance of a system by 25% under a specific load pattern that we're experiencing.

460
00:50:58,470 --> 00:51:06,790
Like we literally had that happen at one point where one CUDA kernel flag improved performance by 25% due to a specific load pattern.

461
00:51:06,790 --> 00:51:14,950
And that's the kind of thing where there's no way we could have ever guessed that going into building the system.

462
00:51:14,950 --> 00:51:26,630
But by observing it, by seeing when it spiked, and then by looking at profiling of, hey, these operations were taking this amount of time, we're able to figure out that that's the thing that we needed to optimize in that situation.

463
00:51:26,640 --> 00:51:33,670
Are there any other lessons learned or best practices around managing latency that you'd like to convey?

464
00:51:33,680 --> 00:51:44,630
I think the most important one is that for anything latency sensitive, everything that's not critical should get removed from the critical path.

465
00:51:44,630 --> 00:51:50,150
And you know, when I phrase it that way, it sounds obvious cuz uh it's called the critical path,

466
00:51:50,150 --> 00:52:00,710
but it's really easy to have a database query here or a blocking operation there that is occurring in the primary path for a latency sensitive application.

467
00:52:00,710 --> 00:52:09,589
But 3 milliseconds here, 5 milliseconds there, 10 milliseconds another place and that's under good conditions, you know, could become 100 milliseconds here or there really fast.

468
00:52:09,599 --> 00:52:22,550
And it's actually often the innocuous pieces of code that people write that can very easily just cause overhead that doesn't need to be there. Right?

469
00:52:22,550 --> 00:52:28,630
That's the easy kind of overhead to avoid when being disciplined about programming.

470
00:52:28,630 --> 00:52:31,750
A lot of the other stuff is like you're gonna have to whack away at it.

471
00:52:31,750 --> 00:52:33,430
It's going to be a hard problem.

472
00:52:33,430 --> 00:52:40,710
You're going to have to dive into logs, observe things, and understand how like kernels work and things of that sort.

473
00:52:40,710 --> 00:52:44,950
And that's the hard work that always needs to happen.

474
00:52:44,950 --> 00:52:55,109
But the best practice of like make sure only the absolute bare minimum needs to happen in any blocking operation on the critical path like that's the kind of golden rule for me.

475
00:52:55,119 --> 00:53:06,390
It sounds very much like an idea that I ran across some years ago under the name of reactive applications where the idea was anything

476
00:53:06,390 --> 00:53:15,829
that you don't need the response in line put it in an async and just let it happen on its own and if you need to know when it finished use an event.

477
00:53:15,839 --> 00:53:19,990
Exactly. Okay.

478
00:53:19,990 --> 00:53:21,750
Well, Sahed thank you so much for this conversation.

479
00:53:21,750 --> 00:53:28,470
Before we wrap up, would you like to direct listeners to anywhere on the internet where they can find either you or your company?

480
00:53:28,480 --> 00:53:32,710
Yeah, so I would just recommend going to the Whisper Flow website.

481
